           daylight saving adjustment is made. See the discussion below, under
           LLOOCCAALL vvss UUTTCC.

           The kernel also keeps a timezone value, the ----hhccttoossyyss function sets
           it to the timezone configured for the system. The system timezone
           is configured by the TTZZ environment variable or the _/_e_t_c_/_l_o_c_a_l_t_i_m_e
           file, as ttzzsseett(3) would interpret them. The obsolete _t_z___d_s_t_t_i_m_e
           field of the kernel’s timezone value is set to zero. (For details
           on what this field used to mean, see sseettttiimmeeooffddaayy(2).)

           When used in a startup script, making the ----hhccttoossyyss function the
           first caller of sseettttiimmeeooffddaayy(2) from boot, it will set the NTP '11
           minute mode' timescale via the _p_e_r_s_i_s_t_e_n_t___c_l_o_c_k___i_s___l_o_c_a_l kernel
           variable. If the Hardware Clock’s timescale configuration is
           changed then a reboot is required to inform the kernel. See the
           discussion below, under AAuuttoommaattiicc HHaarrddwwaarree CClloocckk SSyynncchhrroonniizzaattiioonn bbyy
           tthhee KKeerrnneell.

           This is a good function to use in one of the system startup scripts
           before the file systems are mounted read/write.

           This function should never be used on a running system. Jumping
           system time will cause problems, such as corrupted filesystem
           timestamps. Also, if something has changed the Hardware Clock, like
           NTP’s '11 minute mode', then ----hhccttoossyyss will set the time
           incorrectly by including drift compensation.

           Drift compensation can be inhibited by setting the drift factor in
           _/_e_t_c_/_a_d_j_t_i_m_e to zero. This setting will be persistent as long as
           the ----uuppddaattee--ddrriifftt option is not used with ----ssyyssttoohhcc at shutdown
           (or anywhere else). Another way to inhibit this is by using the
           ----nnooaaddjjffiillee option when calling the ----hhccttoossyyss function. A third
           method is to delete the _/_e_t_c_/_a_d_j_t_i_m_e file. HHwwcclloocckk will then
           default to using the UTC timescale for the Hardware Clock. If the
           Hardware Clock is ticking local time it will need to be defined in
           the file. This can be done by calling hhwwcclloocckk ----llooccaallttiimmee ----aaddjjuusstt;
           when the file is not present this command will not actually adjust
           the Clock, but it will create the file with local time configured,
           and a drift factor of zero.

           A condition under which inhibiting hhwwcclloocckk's drift correction may
           be desired is when dual-booting multiple operating systems. If
           while this instance of Linux is stopped, another OS changes the
           Hardware Clock’s value, then when this instance is started again
           the drift correction applied will be incorrect.

           For hhwwcclloocckk's drift correction to work properly it is imperative
           that nothing changes the Hardware Clock while its Linux instance is
           not running.

